# Why two rational suspects both confess
subtitle: "Why two suspects who each do what is best for them both end up worse off, and how meeting again and again lets cooperation pay."
tags: ["game theory","nash equilibrium","cooperation","repeated games","tit for tat"]
teaches: ["concepts/prisoners-dilemma","concepts/nash-equilibrium","concepts/repeated-game-dynamics","strategies/tit-for-tat"]
thumb: "card orange star"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Zubenelgenubi | a casual, wry game theorist; dry humour, conversational","take":"lines"}

## Why two rational suspects both confess
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Suspect A
    col_label: Suspect B
    row_values:
      - Silent
      - Confess
    col_values:
      - Silent
      - Confess
    cells:
      - ['1, 1', '10, 0']
      - ['0, 10', '5, 5']
    highlight:
      - [1, 1]
adjust:
    row_header_0:
      scale: 1.5
      move: [-1.5, 0]
    row_header_1:
      scale: 1.5
      move: [-1.5, 0]
    col_header_0:
      scale: 1.5
    col_header_1:
      scale: 1.5
    row_title:
      scale: 1.4
      move: [-1.5, 2]
    col_title:
      scale: 1.4
      move: [0, 3]
sources:
    - id: axelrod1980
      title: Effective Choice in the Prisoner's Dilemma
      authors: Robert Axelrod
      year: 1980
      finding: In a computer tournament of iterated prisoner's dilemma strategies, Anatol Rapoport's Tit for Tat, the simplest entry, scored best.

    text r1 "1: both silent" x 885 y 540 font_size 28 hidden true
    text r2 "2: B confesses" x 885 y 460 font_size 28 hidden true
    text r3 "3: A confesses" x 885 y 380 font_size 28 hidden true
    text r4 "4: both silent" x 885 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait portrait_anatol_rapoport cites ["axelrod1980"] x 890 y 400 width 180 of "Anatol Rapoport" cameo true hidden true
    text years "years in prison" #8f887c x 520 y 180 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Why two rational suspects both confess"

Why do two people end up worse off when each does what's best for them? The classic case: two suspects, questioned in separate rooms.
    draw grid row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1

If both stay silent, the police can prove only a small crime: one year each. The numbers are years in prison, A's first.
    draw cell_0_0 years

If one confesses and the other stays silent, the confessor walks free and the other gets ten years.
    draw cell_0_1 cell_1_0 parallel true

If both confess, five years each.
    draw cell_1_1

    pause 0.4

Take A's view. If B stays silent, confessing means walking free instead of one year.
    focus cell_0_0 cell_1_0

If B confesses, confessing means five years instead of ten. Whatever B does, A does better by confessing.
    focus cell_0_1 cell_1_1

B reasons the same way: whatever A does, B does better by confessing too.
    focus cell_0_1 cell_1_1 cell_1_0

So both confess and get five years — the Nash equilibrium — though silence would have cost them one year each.
    draw hl_1_1

Why not promise each other silence? Alone in the room, breaking the promise still pays. Nothing in the table has changed.
    highlight cell_0_0

One way out: meet again and again, as firms, neighbours and countries do.
    draw r1

Round one: both stay silent.
    highlight cell_0_0

    draw r2

Say B is tempted in round two and confesses. A plays tit for tat: start silent, then copy what the other did last round.
    highlight cell_0_1

    draw r3

So in round three A confesses. Betray once, and you are punished the very next round.
    highlight cell_1_0

    draw r4

Go back to silence, and so does the other. Today's betrayal costs tomorrow's cooperation.
    highlight cell_0_0

    erase r1 r2 r3 r4

In Robert Axelrod's 1980 tournaments of computer strategies, this simplest one, sent in by Anatol Rapoport, won.
    draw portrait_anatol_rapoport

    erase portrait_anatol_rapoport

    quiz "The suspects meet once, but talk first and promise to stay silent. What happens?"
        right: Each still does better by confessing: a promise changes no number in the table, and in one meeting there is no next round to punish a betrayal.
        * Both stay silent
        + Each still does better by confessing
        * Only the one who promised first stays silent
